@@ -24,6 +24,10 @@ Submitted jobs receive a delivered worktree snapshot; each interactive command r
 
 Never bound long compute with a Bash transport timeout. Give the job its native maximum, checkpoint resumable progress, trap `SIGTERM`/`SIGINT`, and use `await_slurm_job` to wait and never poll. For on-demand jobs, this wake waits until output flow-back either completes or fails explicitly; a successful wake makes artifact reads and `reuse_volume_from_job_id` safe from an in-progress-upload race. When woken, inspect terminal state and logs before interpreting the result; also inspect the flow-back outcome, then checkpoint any changed result or state.
 
+#### Narrate work in its logs
+
+A job's stdout/stderr is the researcher's live window into the run; silence is indistinguishable from a hang. Every submitted job prints a startup banner naming the phase, run identity (model, data, key hyperparameters), and output destination before slow work begins; a bounded progress line at least every few minutes during long stages (step or item count, key metric, throughput); one line at each phase transition; and an exit summary naming results, counts, and artifact paths. Run Python unbuffered or flush prints so lines appear when written. Log an error with the context that produced it — rank, shard, item, operative config — never as a bare traceback. Interactive commands follow the same rule at command scale: print what the command checks, end with a named result line, and give a longer command the same bounded progress prints; a silently succeeding probe is not evidence. Keep cadence bounded: no per-step spam and no tensor dumps. Telemetry owns progress bars; logs carry the story. `/slurm` owns the line-level recipe.
+
 #### Submission failures and duplicate safety
 
 For ordinary pre-entrypoint infrastructure failures, follow the worker's two-retry policy. Use the paced policy here only when the error begins `Compute cluster unreachable` or a structured receipt explicitly marks a connectivity failure. The platform converts recognized SSH and rsync failures into that classification; do not infer connectivity from an unclassified raw message. A generic delivery failure, local deadline, scheduler resource/partition refusal, or request error is not connectivity. Retry the unchanged payload every few minutes initially, then back off toward hourly with `schedule_wakeup`, ending the turn between attempts. Continue until recovery; do not abandon the retry loop autonomously. After about 30 minutes, write one researcher-facing checkpoint that the cluster is unreachable and automatic retries continue; write no further outage checkpoints until recovery.
